Due to the dynamic nature of javascript creating an object is as simple as the code below.
var myObject = {};
Now if you want to create an object with properties you can modify the above code like below
//var myObject = {
       firstProperty: '',
       secondProperty: 100,
       thirdProperty: []
}

Or add the properties after creating the object
var myObject = {};
In the following section, we will explore all the configuration that is happening in the background at the moment of object creation and try to understand how we can use this configuration option to create objects that fit our requirements.
The generic definition of objects in computer science is value in memory that mostly have identifiers that reference them. In javascript objects can be seen as a collection of properties.
In javascript objects can be seen as a collection of properties.
Javascript objects have two properties.
— are properties that hold the actual value of the object and define the behavior of the property.
Enumerable(boolean: false) — a boolean value that specifies if the property is accessible through iterators like for…in loops, spread operators, Object.keys(). if left undefined it will take on the default value of false.
— 
Value([string|object|number|Array|null]: undefined) — holds the actual value of the property. it can be any valid javascript data type. when we set the value using this property it’s the same as assigning it directly as we saw it earlier. if left undefined it will take on a default value of undefined.
— 
get (Function: undefined) — called whenever we attempt to retrieve the property value associated with this attribute as a function that takes no arguments.
Mutual Exclusivity between Configurations
Value & writable Vs. set & get assertion
you can set data writable & valueproperties and also define set and get accessor since they create conflicts.
But how do we actually set these values did you say? well, that we’re one of the predefined functions for the global Object Object.definePropertiy & Object.defineProperties come in to play.
The global Object provides lots of prefined functions that let us manipulate Objects in various ways and is the superclass to all objects created in javascript.
Object.definePropery and its sibling Object.defineProperties let us configure the object's properties as we want. in the following section, we will see how we can use these functions to configure object properties.
— Object.definePropertytake 3 arguments.
Object.defineProperty(obj: Object, key: strinng, config: Object)
Object — the first argument takes the object to which we want to configure.
